WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:04.700
The workflow you set up, in the previous
lesson, identified

2
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.933
all the fields in the form and generated
plausible answers to all of them.

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.300
But there are a couple of fields
where the answers could be better.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.800
In this lesson,
you will give feedback to the agent

5
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.033
on the returned answers
and ask it to re-answer when necessary.

6
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.833
Let's get to it.

7
00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:23.200
LLMs are amazing, but they are best used
to augment rather than replace a human.

8
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.433
Your current form
filler does an excellent job figuring out

9
00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:29.633
what fields need to be filled
in, and gets most of the fields right.

10
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.900
But there are a couple where it needs
a little help. To take care of those

11
00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:34.800
you're going to create a human in the loop

12
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.800
workflow, where you can optionally provide
feedback to the agent

13
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.800
you've created
and have it incorporated into the results.

14
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:43.500
So let's get started on that.

15
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.800
First, we'll need our imports as usual.

16
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.200
And we'll need asyncio.

17
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.500
And we'll need our API keys.

18
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.100
The changes we're going to make

19
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.300
to our workflow are pretty significant.

20
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.733
We're going to use the input required
and human response event.

21
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.100
These are new special events
specifically designed

22
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.400
to allow you to exit the workflow
and get feedback back into it.

23
00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:10.133
You used to have a single step
which parsed your form and fired off all

24
00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:11.300
the questions.

25
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.400
Since we now might loop back
and ask questions several times,

26
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.133
you don't need to parse the form
every time, so we'll split up those steps.

27
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.933
This kind of refactoring is very common
as you create a more complex workflow.

28
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:25.233
Your new generate questions
step will be triggered either

29
00:01:25.233 --> 00:01:28.233
by a generate questions
event triggered, by the form parser,

30
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:32.800
or by a feedback event, which is the loop
that we'll take after getting feedback.

31
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.900
Then we'll emit an input-required event,
and in the same step

32
00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:39.333
you'll wait for a human response event.

33
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:42.333
This will pause the whole workflow
waiting for outside input.

34
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.100
Finally, you'll use the LLM
to parse the feedback and decide

35
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.700
whether it means you should continue
and output the results,

36
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.333
or if you need to loop back. That's a lot.

37
00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:52.733
So let's get started.

38
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:55.000
First, we bring in our new event types.

39
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.400
And now, as usual, we set up our events.

40
00:02:00.433 --> 00:02:03.100
There's a new one here.
The feedback event.

41
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.533
Let's put in our workflow
and walk through it

42
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:08.533
line by line.

43
00:02:09.333 --> 00:02:10.700
This stuff is all the same.

44
00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:14.200
Your setup remains the same
and your parse form has changed.

45
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.500
Now we've separated the form parsing
from the question generation.

46
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.700
So after we've parsed the form
into fields,

47
00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.600
when in the context,
we set our fields to fill, and then we

48
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.433
send it to the generate questions event.
The generate questions step,

49
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.733
like I said, can be triggered
by a generate questions event,

50
00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:34.700
but it can also be triggered by a feedback
event.

51
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.200
We'll see how that happens in a bit.

52
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.733
The first thing we do is we get the list
of fields to fill from the context.

53
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.600
Then for each of those fields,
we fire off a query event

54
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:48.233
just like we did before,
and we store the total number of fields

55
00:02:48.233 --> 00:02:51.800
so that our collect events will know
how many fields it should be waiting for.

56
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.300
Our Ask Questions event is unchanged,
but now in our fill in application

57
00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.300
form, we're going to emit
an input-required event.

58
00:02:59.633 --> 00:03:02.633
Just like before,
we're going to wait for collect events.

59
00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.833
It will return none until
all of the events have been collected.

60
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.400
And once it's done, we're going to turn it
into a list of questions.

61
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.200
The new thing we're doing here
is we're going to set our sets

62
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.200
of questions
into the context to use later.

63
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.500
And now we're going to emit
an input-required event.

64
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.400
This is going to send a message
to the human asking how things look,

65
00:03:23.733 --> 00:03:26.400
and asking the human to give some response.

66
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.400
In our next step,
which is called get feedback,

67
00:03:29.633 --> 00:03:31.700
we accept a human response event.

68
00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:35.000
So unlike our previous steps,
which were always triggered

69
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.000
by the event from the step before,
we now have a gap.

70
00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.233
The input required event
isn't being emitted by anything.

71
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.400
It's going to have to be emitted later.

72
00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.500
The input required event is being captured
by something else,

73
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.100
and the human response event isn't
being emitted inside of the workflow.

74
00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.600
It has to come from outside the workflow.

75
00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.500
We'll see how that happens in a second.

76
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.500
Once we get the feedback, what we'll do is

77
00:03:57.533 --> 00:04:00.433
we will ask the LLM

78
00:04:00.433 --> 00:04:03.433
to decide whether or not
the feedback is good or bad.

79
00:04:03.733 --> 00:04:08.233
If everything is fine, the LLM will read
the feedback and say that things are okay,

80
00:04:08.333 --> 00:04:11.733
but if there's any other feedback,
it will respond with the word "feedback"

81
00:04:12.033 --> 00:04:15.500
and we will use that to decide
what to do next.

82
00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:20.733
If the LLM said everything was okay, then
we'll emit a stop event and we'll be done.

83
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.733
If the LLM emits feedback then
we are going to emit a feedback event.

84
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:26.900
Cool.

85
00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.833
So now your workflow is ready to get some
feedback, but how do we actually get it?

86
00:04:31.433 --> 00:04:32.200
Turns out

87
00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:37.000
you already know how. The input required
event is an event in the event stream.

88
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.400
Just like progress events
and text events that you sent previously,

89
00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.600
You can intercept it
the same way that you did those, and use

90
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.600
the send event method on the context
to send back a human response event.

91
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:51.733
So all of this happens in the code
where we execute the workflow.

92
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.200
Let's walk through it line by line.

93
00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.500
This part is the same as you've seen
before.

94
00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.500
We get a handler
by executing the workflow.

95
00:04:59.633 --> 00:05:03.700
And this part is filtering the events
just like we did before.

96
00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:07.400
Except this time
we're waiting for an input-required event.

97
00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.300
When we see an input-required event,

98
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.200
we're going to print out the form
so the user can see what's up.

99
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:17.033
And then we're going to use the input
method to get keyboard feedback.

100
00:05:17.233 --> 00:05:20.900
Once we've got the feedback
in the response variable, we'll send it

101
00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.933
as a human response
event using the send event method.

102
00:05:24.833 --> 00:05:27.233
And then will await the handler.

103
00:05:27.233 --> 00:05:30.033
So let's execute this
and see how that looks.

104
00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:31.200
Cool.

105
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.600
So here is the combined list of fields

106
00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.600
and succinct factual answers
that the LLM has given us.

107
00:05:37.833 --> 00:05:41.000
Just like before, it's done
a good job of numbering everything,

108
00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:44.833
but projects portfolio
is still a list of things

109
00:05:44.833 --> 00:05:48.100
that the applicant did
instead of a link to their portfolio.

110
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.400
So let's say that.

111
00:05:52.133 --> 00:05:54.100
Portfolio

112
00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.333
should be

113
00:05:56.333 --> 00:05:59.333
a URL.

114
00:05:59.633 --> 00:06:02.100
The LLM says the verdict was feedback.

115
00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.133
So now that's going to trigger the loop.

116
00:06:04.133 --> 00:06:06.100
It's going to try everything again.

117
00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.533
But we haven't actually found a way
to incorporate the feedback yet.

118
00:06:09.733 --> 00:06:12.800
So it's just going to do everything
exactly the same way it did.

119
00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:16.100
We'll incorporate the feedback
in our next change.

120
00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:19.100
In the meantime,
let's just say this is fine.

121
00:06:19.733 --> 00:06:23.533
The LLM says the verdict was okay
so the workflow halts.

122
00:06:23.933 --> 00:06:24.633
Cool.

123
00:06:24.633 --> 00:06:29.100
So now, let's further modify things
to actually do something useful

124
00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.633
with that feedback
that we've generated. This involves

125
00:06:31.633 --> 00:06:34.800
checking if there is feedback
and appending it to the questions.

126
00:06:34.933 --> 00:06:36.600
In this simplified example,

127
00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.133
we're going to append the feedback
to every question in case it's relevant.

128
00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:42.133
But a more sophisticated agent

129
00:06:42.133 --> 00:06:45.300
might apply it to only the fields
where the feedback applied.

130
00:06:45.733 --> 00:06:47.900
Here's our modified workflow.

131
00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.300
Setup is the same.

132
00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:52.700
Parse form is the same,
but generate questions has changed.

133
00:06:52.833 --> 00:06:57.833
Now we're going to check if the attribute
feedback exists on our event.

134
00:06:58.800 --> 00:06:59.700
If the attribute

135
00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.000
feedback exists on our event,
that means that it's a feedback event

136
00:07:03.233 --> 00:07:04.300
and we're going to use it.

137
00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:08.200
So we're going to modify each question
that applies to each field

138
00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.133
to include the feedback.

139
00:07:10.133 --> 00:07:11.933
We're going to say
it might not be relevant

140
00:07:11.933 --> 00:07:15.100
to this particular field,
so that the LLM doesn't get confused

141
00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.800
about why we're applying
feedback to every single event.

142
00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:20.933
Then, just like before,
we generate query events

143
00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:25.433
and we store the total number of fields.
Ask question remains the same.

144
00:07:25.433 --> 00:07:29.433
Fill in application remains the same
and get feedback remains the same.

145
00:07:29.733 --> 00:07:31.833
So let's execute this cell.

146
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.000
And then run our workflow.

147
00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.133
Everything here is exactly the same
as it was last time,

148
00:07:42.133 --> 00:07:45.200
except now it's going to understand
what to do with the feedback.

149
00:07:45.733 --> 00:07:48.733
Okay,
we've got the first attempt of the form.

150
00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.233
As before, project

151
00:07:50.233 --> 00:07:53.333
portfolio is a description
of the things that the applicant has done.

152
00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.800
Now we'll give it the feedback.

153
00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.800
Portfolio should be a URL.

154
00:07:59.933 --> 00:08:00.933
We hit enter.

155
00:08:00.933 --> 00:08:03.933
The LLM has worked out
that the verdict was feedback.

156
00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.033
Now it's going to try again.

157
00:08:06.033 --> 00:08:07.633
And it's going to append that feedback

158
00:08:07.633 --> 00:08:11.033
to every single question
so that the LLM knows what to do.

159
00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:12.533
Cool.

160
00:08:12.533 --> 00:08:15.033
Here's the new modified version.

161
00:08:15.033 --> 00:08:17.833
And as you can see field six

162
00:08:17.833 --> 00:08:20.833
the portfolio has correctly been turned
into a URL.

163
00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:22.633
That's exactly what we wanted.

164
00:08:22.633 --> 00:08:24.833
So let's tell it. That's great.

165
00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.733
One of the fun things about
giving feedback in natural language is

166
00:08:28.733 --> 00:08:31.100
you can just say anything
and it'll figure out what you meant.

167
00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:34.233
The LLM says that's great
means the verdict was okay.

168
00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.400
And so it stops the workflow
and gives us the output.

169
00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:38.500
Success!

170
00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.000
Our agent now responds to human

171
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.600
in the loop feedback and produces
more accurate filled forms as a result.

172
00:08:43.933 --> 00:08:44.900
Congratulations.

